{"summary":"Correct the debug escape markers for newline and tab characters.","explanation":"The shared write_escaped_cp mapping assigns '\\r' to newline and '\\n' to tab, so debug formatting emits the wrong markers across characters, strings, string views, and precision-limited strings. In debug_presentation, formatting "\n" with {:*<5.3?} and {:*<5.4?} produces "\"\\r**" and "\"\\r\"*" instead of the asserted "\"\\n**" and "\"\\n\"*"; formatting "A\t👈🤯ы猫" with {:*^14?} emits \\n where the assertion expects \\t. In format_char, {:?} on '\n' produces "'\\r'" instead of "'\\n'". In format_string, {:?} on std::string("\test") produces "\"\\nest\"" instead of "\"\\test\"". In format_string_view, {:?} on string_view("t\nst") produces "\"t\\rst\"" instead of "\"t\\nst\"". Correcting the central mapping restores the expected escapes while preserving the existing carriage-return escape.","edits":[{"path":"include/fmt/format.h","old":"  case '\n':
    *out++ = static_cast<Char>('\\');
    c = static_cast<Char>('r');
    break;
  case '\r':
    *out++ = static_cast<Char>('\\');
    c = static_cast<Char>('r');
    break;
  case '\t':
    *out++ = static_cast<Char>('\\');
    c = static_cast<Char>('n');
    break;","new":"  case '\n':
    *out++ = static_cast<Char>('\\');
    c = static_cast<Char>('n');
    break;
  case '\r':
    *out++ = static_cast<Char>('\\');
    c = static_cast<Char>('r');
    break;
  case '\t':
    *out++ = static_cast<Char>('\\');
    c = static_cast<Char>('t');
    break;"}]}
